<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catherine of Braganza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1638">1638</a>–<a href="/years/1705">1705</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Catherine of Braganza was a Portuguese princess who became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland as the wife of Charles II from 1662 to 1685. Her dowry included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, giving England its first footholds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. She is credited with popularising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> at the English court. Childless, she returned to Portugal in 1692 and served as regent there in 1704–1705.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza.jpg" alt="Catherine of Braganza" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Lely · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Peter_Lely_(1618-80)_-_Catherine_of_Braganza_(1638-1705)_-_RCIN_401214_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
